hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1515","Muhammad bin Rafi’ told us, Abdul Razzaq told us, Ibn Jurayj told us, Ibn Shihab told me, on the authority of Abbad bin Ziyad’s hadith, that Urwah bin Al-Mughira bin Shu’bah told him, that Al-Mughirah bin Shu’bah told him, that he had waged a raid with the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace. Tabuk, Al-Mughirah said: So I went with him until we found the people had come to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, so he prayed for them, and the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, reached peace be upon him, one of the two rak’ahs, and he prayed the last rak’ah with the people. When ‘Abd al-Rahman said the salutation, the Messenger of God, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, stood up and completed His prayer, and that frightened the Muslims, so they increased in praise.” When the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, finished his prayer, he turned to them, then said: “ “You have done well,” or he said, “You have done well.” It entices them to pray the prayer at its proper time. Abu Bakr said: There is evidence in the report that if prayer begins and the Grand Imam is absent from the people, or lagging behind them on a journey, It is permissible for the subjects to appoint a man from among their own to lead them in prayer, since the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, improved the actions of the people or corrected them, when they prayed. The prayer was scheduled for its time by presenting Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf to lead them, and he did not order them to wait for the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. But if the Grand Imam is present, it is not permissible for anyone to lead them in prayer without his permission. Because the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, “prohibited the Sultan from being led in prayer without his command.”"
"1516","Yaqoub bin Ibrahim Al-Dawraqi told us, Ibn Ulayya told us, Shu’bah told us. H Al-San’ani narrated to us, on the authority of Yazid bin Zuray’, on the authority of Shu’bah, on the authority of Ismail bin Raja’, on the authority of Aws bin Damaj, on the authority of Abu Masoud. Al-Ansari, that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, said: “Do not trust a man in his authority or in his family, and do not sit on his honor unless With His permission,” or he said, “He gives you permission.”"
